// Formative practice quiz for routing-and-route-parameters. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/effects-routing-and-rendering/routing-and-route-parameters

::routing-and-route-parameters o1 - outlet role::A layout route has child routes, but only the layout appears at a child URL. What is missing?{
=An Outlet element in the layout component#Correct. Outlet marks where the matched child renders.
~A key on the child routes#Not quite. Keys are for lists. Routes do not need them for this.
~A second Routes element in the child#Not quite. The nesting is already configured. The layout just needs an Outlet.
~A page reload#Not quite. Reloading changes nothing, because the child still has nowhere to render.
}

::routing-and-route-parameters o1 - index route::What does an index route render?{
=A default child at the parent route URL#Correct. It fills the Outlet when no other child matches at the parent URL.
~The list of all routes#Not quite. It is a route for a default child, not a listing.
~The index.html file#Not quite. The name is similar but the idea is unrelated.
~Only the home page of the whole site#Not quite. Any parent route can have an index child.
}

::routing-and-route-parameters o2 - params are strings::At the URL /items/42, with the route items/\:id, what does id hold after reading useParams?{
=The string 42#Correct. URL values are text, so convert with Number when you need a number.
~The number 42#Not quite. React Router does not convert params to numbers.
~The whole path /items/42#Not quite. Only the matched segment is captured.
~undefined, until an Effect runs#Not quite. The value is available during render.
}

::routing-and-route-parameters o3 - catch-all::How do you show a not-found page for any URL with no matching route?{
=Add a Route with path set to an asterisk that renders the page#Correct. The splat path matches whatever no other route matched.
~Add a Route with path 404#Not quite. That only matches the literal URL /404.
~Check location inside every component#Not quite. A single catch-all route is far simpler.
~Nothing, because the browser shows one automatically#Not quite. In a single-page app the router decides what renders, so you provide this page yourself.
}
